    "case_id": "219",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 60s presented with multiple dome-shaped, skin-colored asymptomatic papules located on the face and vulva. The lesions first appeared around 50 years of age and gradually increased to approximately 60 lesions. Medical history was significant for a meningioma. Family history was notable for a daughter with similar facial papules that had not undergone biopsy.\n\nMultiple dome-shaped, skin-colored papules present on the central face and around the eyes, with similar papules noted on the vulva. No palmar or plantar pits were observed.\n\n{'Histopathology': 'Eight lesions underwent biopsy, showing buds and cords of basaloid cells enclosing small cystic spaces. Clefting between tumor aggregates and the surrounding stroma was focally evident.', 'Imaging': 'Radiographic studies did not reveal any jaw cysts.', 'Genetic Testing': 'Whole-exome sequencing revealed:    - A conserved splice-site mutation in SUFU gene (c.757-2A>G) in all tumor and normal tissue samples    - Additional distinct deletions of the trans SUFU allele in all tumor samples    - No deletions present in the normal sample', 'Images': {'Title': 'Clinical and Histopathologic Features', 'Description': 'Shows numerous small, domed, skin-colored papules on the central face and around the eyes, close-up appearance of tumors, and photomicrographs of infundibulocystic lesions'}}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Multiple hereditary infundibulocystic basal cell carcinoma syndrome (MHIBCC)\nB. Infundibulocystic basal cell carcinoma\nC. Basal cell nevus syndrome (Gorlin syndrome)\nD. Gorlin Syndrome (Basal Cell Nevus Syndrome)\n",
